Now the widget tests: login page, home page, and app-level widget_test.dart.

LoginPage API design:
```dart
LoginPage({required SessionController controller})
``
It renders:
- Title "Sign in to continue" / app name
- GoogleSignInButton (custom, key: const Key('google_sign_in_button'))
- Apple: `SignInWithAppleButton` from package shown only when Platform.isIOS/isMacOS — but in widget tests `dart:io Platform` doesn't work on non-mac hosts. Use `defaultTargetPlatform` instead — overidable in tests via `debugDefaultTargetPlatformOverride`. I'll create a helper `showAppleSignIn = defaultTargetPlatform == TargetPlatform.iOS || == TargetPlatform.macOS`.
- Remember me: `Switch` or `CheckboxListTile` — with key `remember_me_toggle`. Use `SwitchListTile` with title "Remember me".
- Error text if controller.error != null (snackbar or inline text). Inline text with key `login_error_text`.

Loading state: while busy → buttons disabled + progress indicator (key `login_progress`).

HomePage API:
```dart
HomePage({required SessionController controller})
``
- AppBar with title, sign-out IconButton / button (key `sign_out_button`, or icon Icons.logout)
- Shows displayName/email, avatar initial.

App-level (widget_test.dart):
```dart
DmToolsApp({SessionController? controller}) — if null, builds default with real services.
``
Actually to inject, let DmToolsApp take `authService`/`sessionStore` optionals and build the controller. In tests, construct SessionController with fakes and pass it.

Widget test flow for login page:
- pumpWidget(MaterialApp(home: LoginPage(controller: controller)))
- expect find.byKey google button findsOneWidget; remember toggle exists (value false)
- Apple hidden on android: debugDefaultTargetPlatformOverride = TargetPlatform.android
- Apple visible on iOS: override iOS
- tap toggle → true; tap google → expect controller method call (use FakeAuthService and check googleSignInCalls == 1 and store saved)

LoginPage calls controller.signInWithGoogle(remember: _rememberMe). After async completion, the app layer rebuilds (ListenableBuilder) and shows HomePage. In LoginPage standalone test, check FakeAuthService.googleSignInCalls and store state.

For tapping, the button press triggers async work → use `await tester.pumpAndSetle()`.

Home page test:
- shows user email; tap sign-out → signOutCalls == 1, store cleared.

App-level test (real MyApp behavior — restore then route):
1. No remembered session → after pumpAndSetle, login page visible; home not.
2. Remembered session → home visible immediately (login skipped).
3. From remembered session, tap sign-out → login page back, store load() → null.

For app-level, I need `DmToolsApp` to accept an injected controller. Design:

```dart
class DmToolsApp extends StatefulWidget {
 const DmToolsApp({super.key, this.sessionController});
 final SessionController? sessionController;
 ...
}
``
In state: `late SessionController _controller = widget.sessionController ?? SessionController(authService: GoogleAppleAuthService(), sessionStore: SharedPreferencesSessionStore());` — but SharedPreferences.getInstance() is async; SharedPreferencesSessionStore constructor takes `prefs` future? Simpler: make `SharedPreferencesSessionStore` create its own instance lazily: factory `SharedPreferencesSessionStore.create()` async... Hmm, but restore happens initState → can be async: `_controller.restore()` is async; the store's load() can `await SharedPreferences.getInstance()` internally. So design SharedPreferencesSessionStore to fetch the instance itself:

```dart
class SharedPreferencesSessionStore implements SessionStore {
 SharedPreferencesSessionStore({SharedPreferences? prefs}) : _prefsOverride = prefs;
 SharedPreferences? _prefsOverride;
 Future<SharedPreferences> _prefs() async => _prefsOverride ??= await SharedPreferences.getInstance();
``

But then the unit test can't inject mocks... SharedPreferences.setMockInitialValues works with getInstance in tests, so the default path works in tests too. But injecting the instance is still nice. Keep optional `prefs` param as I wrote in the test (`prefs: await SharedPreferences.getInstance()`). Both work.

In widget tests with the real store, `SharedPreferences.setMockInitialValues({})` is required. For app-level tests, use in-memory store + fake auth. Good.

Now — the `restore()` being called in DmToolsApp initState. Status starts at `restoring`, so splash is shown. 

Also `main()` in main.dart stays thin:

```dart
void main() {
 runApp(DmToolsApp());
}
``
DmToolsApp default constructor builds real deps. But careful: constructing GoogleAppleAuthService in field initializer is fine (lazy).

GoogleAppleAuthService impl:

```dart
class GoogleAppleAuthService implements AuthService {
 GoogleAppleAuthService({GoogleSignIn? googleSignIn}) : _googleSignIn = googleSignIn ?? GoogleSignIn.instance;
 final GoogleSignIn _googleSignIn;
 bool _googleInitialized = false;

 Future<void> _ensureGoogleInitialized() async {
   if (!_googleInitialized) {
     await _googleSignIn.initialize();
     _googleInitialized = true;
   }
 }

 @override
 Future<AppUser?> signInWithGoogle() async {
   await _ensureGoogleInitialized();
   try {
     final account = await _googleSignIn.authenticate(scopeHint: const ['email']);
     return AppUser(id: account.id, provider: AuthProvider.google, displayName: account.displayName, email: account.email);
   } on GoogleSignInException catch (e) {
     if (e.code == GoogleSignInExceptionCode.canceled) return null;
     rethrow;
   }
 }

 @override
 Future<AppUser?> signInWithApple() async {
   try {
     final credential = await SignInWithApple.getAppleIDCredential(
       scopes: [AppleIDAuthorizationScopes.fullName, AppleIDAuthorizationScopes.email],
     );
     final displayName = [credential.givenName, credential.familyName].whereType<String>().where((s) => s.trim().isNotEmpty).join(' ');
     return AppUser(
       id: credential.userIdentifier ?? '', // hmm, nullable
       provider: AuthProvider.apple,
       displayName: displayName.isEmpty ? null : displayName,
       email: credential.email,
     );
   } on SignInWithAppleAuthorizationException catch (e) {
     if (e.code == AuthorizationErrorCode.canceled) return null;
     rethrow;
   }
 }

 @override
 Future<void> signOut() async {
   if (_googleInitialized) {
     await _googleSignIn.signOut();
   }
 }
``

`userIdentifier` nullable: on iOS always present, on Android absent. Use `credential.userIdentifier ?? credential.identityToken ?? ''`... For an empty id, falling back to identityToken is reasonable. Keep simple: `userIdentifier ?? ''`.

Check AuthorizationErrorCode values in sign_in_with_apple 8.2: `canceled`, `failed`, `invalidResponse`, `notHandled`, `unknown`. I'll verify.

Platform check for apple availability — put in a small function `bool get appleSignInAvailable => defaultTargetPlatform == TargetPlatform.iOS || defaultTargetPlatform == TargetPlatform.macOS;` in ui or services.

